#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "912513d9-36c5-54a6-b80a-8e7c22e0b28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386812} {:answer/id #uuid "da835ee7-a6b3-5c39-9c4a-b2b396ae70b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386813} {:answer/id #uuid "b0144868-e729-5eb3-a24a-fe25aa94aaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386814} {:answer/id #uuid "43c712ec-b1a7-5e9e-bcf1-6d721746d495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386815} {:answer/id #uuid "50e7bca9-8e0b-557b-a2ab-04800a8a8fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43c712ec-b1a7-5e9e-bcf1-6d721746d495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386815} :answer-field/id #uuid "061b4845-3db8-5a6d-8a92-4c57275c9f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3af84c3d-711b-5e23-a347-b035f74ed8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161800" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdc5be7-2739-5d72-8645-a79b8e8019e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386819} {:answer/id #uuid "a27dc314-6b68-544b-a3b4-c482196b8698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386820} {:answer/id #uuid "22c05e59-9b2a-58e7-be55-4e4b0efc79de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386821} {:answer/id #uuid "8e93bd31-0382-53bd-b22a-7ef3566d169f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386822} {:answer/id #uuid "0a053b1f-fc32-538b-958d-d2523cadecdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e93bd31-0382-53bd-b22a-7ef3566d169f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386822} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf703d35-eb69-5357-9481-35345da52588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22152fbb-ef80-5520-9e15-6ee765aeb910" :question/math-academy-id "q-161802" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c3ca069-e60e-5f1a-8d5e-3ad3541fa855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386826} {:answer/id #uuid "1e956603-5c9d-519c-914b-867a972e6aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386827} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cbda81-42cf-5a95-b57e-9afbd7ca5712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386828} {:answer/id #uuid "ff93fa7d-1e23-59ae-815c-160a75f52ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386829} {:answer/id #uuid "c724d3ae-0ac9-5e1a-b678-e66fdc97999f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7cbda81-42cf-5a95-b57e-9afbd7ca5712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386828} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90c68ec-18c8-5659-b3a4-9cda8f219fc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2aaddf4-3078-55a7-a4be-d150f83c266d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161812" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3135b2-efe6-553c-80b1-933736a0e6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127923} {:answer/id #uuid "a095503c-4b7a-5e94-a211-d1b3325895fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127924} {:answer/id #uuid "1d68629c-6eeb-5cea-a136-da06c6a6bc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127925} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9df6a6-c528-5fc3-8796-e581fc586f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127926} {:answer/id #uuid "5a71e826-9279-571d-a0a8-bf7f580a8294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a71e826-9279-571d-a0a8-bf7f580a8294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127927} :answer-field/id #uuid "2410315c-b430-54de-94c7-8ff1989bd57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ad6bc33-7c9a-5d03-8204-18b81f486eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39669" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=-x+3y-2z,\\,w=3y+4z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(12+6)-1(-4-0) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c8602a-7bb4-5a10-9252-4bab66b9e350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386832} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0de32f-3a73-5ead-ac33-f8e17fb85ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386833} {:answer/id #uuid "a64ba893-801b-5fe5-9603-e79dd3301e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder ${w}^{2}=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386834} {:answer/id #uuid "ac63c9b6-8b8d-549b-b277-85bfd0761dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386835} {:answer/id #uuid "f87cee29-5861-5d46-9ff2-4a041e787e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32c8602a-7bb4-5a10-9252-4bab66b9e350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386832} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f4cbd02-5ab4-523c-a82f-c7f1b85fce78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab44c8de-700d-51cc-ab4a-3755f7926aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-39709" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}]}
